The app architecture I setup is based **loosely** on the official [recommended app architecture](https://developer.android.com/topic/architecture). In the folder structure, `ui` will hold all the code responsible for the ui and `data` will hold all the classes related to data such as the model classes and repository classes (explained more below). This is so that we can have better [separation of concerns](https://help.sap.com/doc/abapdocu_753_index_htm/7.53/en-US/abenseperation_concerns_guidl.htm#:~:text=Separation%20of%20concerns%20is%20a,and%20arrangement%20in%20software%20layers.) and cleaner code.

To summarize some of the concepts quickly if you don't want to read the docs:
- [**ViewModel**](https://developer.android.com/topic/libraries/architecture/viewmodel). Instead of saving states in fragment / activity itself, you save it into a ViewModel class which you instantiate the onCreate of a fragment / activity. ViewModel also contains [ui logic](https://developer.android.com/topic/architecture/ui-layer/stateholders#logic).
- [**LiveData**](https://developer.android.com/topic/libraries/architecture/livedata). A wrapper for our data in ViewModel that makes it so that we can just suscribe to the changes and make it update the ui automically instead of having to get the data manually after making changes.
- **Repositories**. Repository classes are the only classes that connects to Firebase and exposes the data to ViewModel. Our ui in fragments / activities and ViewModels should never call Firebase directly. Repository classes contains [business logic](https://developer.android.com/topic/architecture/ui-layer/stateholders#logic) such as adding a QR Code to database.
- [**View binding**](https://developer.android.com/topic/libraries/view-binding). It basically replaces findViewById with [better features](https://developer.android.com/topic/libraries/view-binding#findviewbyid). Example:

```
// Using findViewById
class exampleFragment{
	EditText stationNameInput;
	EditText dateInput;
	RadioGroup fuelTypeRadioGroup;
	EditText fuelAmountInput;
	EditText pricePerLitreInput;
	Button saveButton;
	Button deleteButton;

	public View onCreateView(...){
		View dialogView = inflater.inflate(R.layout.visit_dialog, null);
	
		stationNameInput = dialogView.findViewById(R.id.stationNameInput);
                dateInput = dialogView.findViewById(R.id.dateInput);
                fuelTypeRadioGroup = dialogView.findViewById(R.id.fuelTypeRadioGroup);
                fuelAmountInput = dialogView.findViewById(R.id.fuelAmountInput);
                pricePerLitreInput = dialogView.findViewById(R.id.pricePerLitreInput);
    }
}
```
will turn into:
